1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. c3 d5 4. Bb5 {It is a moot point whether this or [Qa4]
be the better move (HS)} dxe4 5. Nxe5 Qd5 6. Qa4 Bd7 7. Nxd7 Kxd7 8. O-O Bd6 9.
d4 ({Mr Parratt thought afterwards he should have played} 9. f4 {, the move in
the text enabling his opponent to get up a strong attack on the kingside (HS)})
9... Qh5 10. g3 (10. h3 $16) 10... Nf6 11. Kg2 {A move of no utility whatever,
except to the enemy (HS)} Qf3+ 12. Kg1 h5 13. Nd2 Qg4 14. c4 $2 Qh3 (14... h4
$1 $19) 15. Re1 h4 ({The following line of play looks much superior:} 15... Ng4
16. Nf1 h4 {- he now threatens to take the [h-pawn] with his knight, and it is
difficult to see any good defence to the assault thus obtained (HS)}) 16. Nf1
hxg3 $2 (16... Ng4 $1 $19) 17. fxg3 Bxg3 18. Re2 Bxh2+ {Mr de Soyres has lost
ground by prosecuting his attack too impetuously. After the sacrifice of this
piece he no longer has the advantage he had (HS)} 19. Rxh2 Qg4+ 20. Rg2 Qf3 21.
Ng3 $2 ({Menacing to mate by} 21. Z0 Rh1+ 22. Kxh1 Qxf1+ 23. Rg1 Rh8+ {(HS)})
21... Ng4 (21... Rh3 {has been suggested as the better move (HS)}) 22. d5 Nf2
$2 ({Another mistaken move. By playing} 22... Ne3 {, he could still have given
his adversary some trouble. For suppose} 23. Bxe3 (23. dxc6+ Ke6) ({or} 23.
Bxc6+ Kd8 {accordingly; and it is not at all clear that Dark Blue can do more
than draw the game (HS)}) 23... Qxe3+ 24. Kf1 Qf3+ 25. Rf2 {He must do this or
consent to a drawn game, we believe (HS)} Qxg3 {and, with his king so exposed,
it is very questionable whether the first player could possibly win the game 
(HS)}) 23. Bxc6+ Kd8 24. Bg5+ {It is this move, bringing the Q's Rook to the
rescue, which the Cambridge champion had most to fear, and which he might have
prevented by playing [22...Ne3] (HS)} f6 25. Rxf2 Qxg3+ 26. Rg2 Qh3 27. Re1 $2
(27. Qb3 $18) 27... fxg5 28. Qa3 Qxa3 29. bxa3 bxc6 30. dxc6 Ke7 31. Rxg5 Kf6
32. Rd5 Rhe8 33. Rd7 Rac8 34. Rf1+ Kg6 35. Rff7 e3 36. Rxg7+ Kf5 37. Rgf7+ Ke4
38. Kf1 Rb8 39. Rfe7+ Kf3 40. Rf7+ Ke4 41. Rfe7+ Rxe7 42. Rxe7+ Kd3 43. Rd7+
Kxc4 44. Rxc7 Kd3 45. Rd7+ Ke4 46. Rb7 Rh8 47. Rxa7 Rh1+ 48. Ke2 Rh2+ 49. Ke1
Rh1+ 50. Ke2 Rh2+ 51. Kf1 Rxa2 ({If he had now played} 51... Kd5 {, he would
have rendered it more difficult for his antagonist to win the game (HS)}) 52.
Re7+ Kf3 53. Rf7+ Ke4 54. c7 Ra1+ 55. Kg2 Ra2+ 56. Kg3 Rc2 57. Re7+ Kd3 58. a4
Kd2 $2 ({The decisive error:} 58... e2 59. Kf2 Rc6 $11 {(JS)}) 59. Rd7+ Ke1 60.
a5 e2 61. a6 Kf1 62. Rf7+ Kg1 {"According to the agreement, Herr Steinitz
examined the position (which we give on a diagram), and gave his decision in
favour of the Oxford champion." (Huddersfield College Magazine, p155) Sources:
Huddersfield College Magazine 1873, p155; Illustrated London News, 3 May 1873;
BCM, March 1973, p137 (RN Coles, One Hundred Years Ago). Notes on the final
position by JS. Notes followed by HS are by Howard Staunton in the ILN.} (62...
Kg1 63. Re7 Rxc7 (63... Kf1 64. a7 Rxc7 65. a8=Q Rxe7 66. Qf8+ ({Steinitz
missed} 66. Qh1# {, as pointed out by Huddersfield College Magazine.})) (63...
Rc3+ 64. Kf4 Kf2 65. Rxe2+ Kxe2 66. a7 Rxc7 67. a8=Q $18) 64. Rxe2 $18 {
Steinitz adjudicated a win for White}) 1-0

