1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. d4 exd4 4. Bc4 Bb4+ 5. c3 dxc3 6. O-O c2 7. Qxc2 d6 8.
a3 Ba5 9. b4 Bb6 10. Bb2 Nf6 11. Nbd2 h6 12. b5 Ne7 13. e5 Nh5 14. Rae1 d5 15.
Bd3 Be6 16. Qa4 g5 17. Nd4 Rf8 18. Nxe6 fxe6 19. Qg4 Nf4 20. Bc2 Qd7 21. a4 Ba5
22. Rd1 O-O-O 23. Ne4 Nf5 24. Nf6 (24. Ba3 {very tempting here; but, in
reality, it is not a good move. For suppose:} h5 25. Qf3 (25. Qxg5 Nh3+ 26.
gxh3 Rg8 {and Black ought to win easily}) 25... dxe4 {and, play as White can,
he must be at a disadvantage.}) 24... Qe7 25. Bxf5 exf5 26. Qxf5+ Kb8 27. Qc2
Qe6 28. Rb1 $2 d4 29. Bxd4 $2 {[The last two moves of White are beyond our
fathom. His game, however, was irrecoverable, so they mattered not much.]} Rxd4
30. Qc5 Rfd8 {Sources: Illustrated London News (Staunton), 12 Apr 1873;
Westminster Papers, 1 Apr 1873} 0-1

